  "verified_against": {
    "source": "release-manifest.json",
    "agrees": true,
    "note": "Hashes were downloaded from the published v5.34.0 release assets and hashed independently, then checked again by re-fetching the URL this pin itself names. Only the pegasus artifact changed: install.sh and the two builder scripts are byte-identical to v5.28.0 and earlier. v5.34.0 changes no line under the engine's src/ at all: the artifact differs from v5.33.0 only in its version stamp. What was wrong was what the engine promised, not what it did, and the whole release moves in one direction on purpose -- the document is made to say the truth, and whatever is not good about that truth is written down as a known limitation rather than dressed up as a guarantee kept. Nothing was repaired by changing logic so a promise would come true. Three promises had stopped holding. An edit of your own to a file the engine installed does not survive: the inclusion contract said an artifact is only updated or removed if it is still unchanged by the user, and that stopped being true when the footprint stopped being permission. The manual repeated the same promise in the worst place, the section a person reads immediately before running uninstall. Writing the limitation surfaced two holes in the warning that no audit had found: appended list items are outside it by construction, and uninstall has no equivalent warning at all. The approved lists named three MCP servers while five ship, and omitted two plugins, in a document that demands the distributed list match the approved one -- so as written every release since Context7 failed its own gate. Corrected, with the structural problem recorded: a hand-kept list inside a document that gates releases will drift again, and what would close it is deriving it from the tree, which does not exist. And the architecture document described a command surface that is not there: a heading asserting no update command exists, a list naming five of nine commands, and an artifact count that is off by nineteen. For this distribution the consequence is structural rather than functional. Everything known is now in two registers instead of one -- a debt is something still to decide or do, an accepted limitation is how the product works on purpose -- and merging them silently promised a fix for things that will never get one. This repository adopted the same split in its own README. Public documents stopped copying and now point, and the link checker was taught to validate the section anchor and not just the file, without which the centralisation would have expired at the first renamed heading. This distribution changed nothing of its own beyond that alignment, as in 1.6.0 through 1.10.0: the work happened upstream and only the pin moved."
  }
}
